WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.512
A scheduler runs a task every 100 ms.

2
00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:08.656
Most runs take 10 ms.

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.599
There are two ways to repeat it.

4
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:15.788
After each run, the executor queues the task again, with a new due time.

5
00:00:15.788 --> 00:00:18.456
Fixed rate: the old due time plus 100.

6
00:00:18.456 --> 00:00:21.335
Fixed delay: the end of the run plus 100.

7
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:23.351
Run 2 is slow.

8
00:00:23.351 --> 00:00:25.429
It takes 250 ms.

9
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.997
Run 3 was due at 200, and run 4 at 300.

10
00:00:28.997 --> 00:00:31.000
Both times have passed.

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.916
When does run 3 start?

12
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.545
At 350, right away.

13
00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:41.369
A due time in the past means: run as soon as a thread is free.

14
00:00:41.369 --> 00:00:45.881
Run 4 is overdue too, so it starts at 360, straight after.

15
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.079
Run 5, due at 400, is on time again.

16
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:53.972
That is fixed rate: overdue runs go back to back until it catches up.

17
00:00:53.972 --> 00:00:56.085
Runs of one task never overlap.

18
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.843
Fixed delay has no timetable.

19
00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:06.307
Run 3 starts 100 ms after the slow run ends, at 460, and everything after it shifts.

20
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:10.591
Use fixed rate for a heartbeat, where the timing is the point.

21
00:01:10.591 --> 00:01:16.808
Use fixed delay for a call that can slow down, like an FX provider: no burst of catch-up calls.

22
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.707
Now the trap.

23
00:01:18.707 --> 00:01:22.891
That re-queue step happens only when a run returns normally.

24
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:28.048
A health check runs every 100 ms, and its third run throws.

25
00:01:28.048 --> 00:01:29.736
What happens at 300?

26
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:33.190
Nothing, ever again.

27
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.273
The exception goes into the ScheduledFuture, and nobody reads it.

28
00:01:37.273 --> 00:01:41.105
The task never goes back in the queue, and nothing is logged.

29
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:45.689
The demo prints three health checks, then silence, and isDone is true.

30
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:51.309
The fix: catch inside the task, so no exception reaches the scheduler.

31
00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:53.763
Log it, and the schedule keeps going.

32
00:01:54.533 --> 00:02:00.471
Fixed rate catches up, fixed delay shifts, and a periodic task that throws stops for good.

33
00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:02.065
Check yourself.

34
00:02:02.065 --> 00:02:04.116
Fixed delay of 100 ms.

35
00:02:04.116 --> 00:02:07.099
A run starts at 0 and takes 250.

36
00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:09.803
When does the next one start?

37
00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:16.015
350: the end of the run, plus 100.
